==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{proto|Germanic|þurnuz|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|(s)tern-|lang=ang}}, a variant of the base ''*(s)ter-, *(s)terə-'' ‘rigid, stiff’.  Germanic cognates include Old Saxon {{term|thorn|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|doorn|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|thorn|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Dorn|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|þorn|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|törne|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃|lang=got|sc=Goth}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Old Church Slavonic {{term|трьнъ|lang=cu}} (Russian {{term|тёрн||sloe, blackthorn|lang=ru}}), Sanskrit {{term|तृण||grass|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}.

